  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n    We aim to improve upon the previous program's performance of 1.507014 by designing a constructive method that explicitly targets minimizing the ratio of the maximum convolution peak to the square of the sum. The key idea is that a uniform distribution maximizes the ratio, so we should optimize sequences that decay towards the interior. \n\n    Different from the LP-based Frank-Wolfe method, we use an incentive-based approach where sequences evolve through structured mutations (edge and center decays). This simplifies computation while potentially escaping local optima. We'll search across lengths and combine decaying sequences to balance peak suppression and length optimization.\n\n    Steps:\n    1. Use base sequences with one-sided normalization (edge decayed).\n    2. Combine base sequences with time-shifted copies.\n    3. Locally optimize these combinations using gradient descent realization.\n    4. Explore lengths from 64 to 1024.\n\n    Mathematical specification\n    Let a = (a_1, ..., a_n) be coefficients in [0,1000] (nonnegative) and normalized to sum \u221a(2n).\n\n    We minimize: J(a) = (2*n * max(convolve(a,a,mode=\"full\"))) / (sum(a))^2\n\n    Convolution indices: \n    - Full convolution mode produces a vector of length 2n-1.\n    - Consider only the positive half to account for symmetry: convolution at position k is defined for k from 0 to n-1, centered at (n-1)/2.\n\n    Search strategy:\n    - Generate base sequences with predefined decay profiles (edge1 and edge2).\n    - Create candidates by combining base sequences with time-delays (W-shaped candidates).\n    - Maintain n=512 as primary because:\n      \u2022 Conv 1800's default used 256,384,512\n      \u2022 n=512 is 2^9, which has efficient FFT properties\n      \u2022 Our evaluation function scales as O(n log n) for FFT-constrained lengths\n\n    Implementation must:\n    1. Use proper mode=\"full\" convolution\n    2. Remove any symmetry-breaking